  "answer": "**Final status: passed.** The second verification run (`tmp/verification/20261021T142402Z-c047dc`) passed build, unit and component. One fix cycle was enough.\n\n**What failed:** The first run passed build but failed the unit stage with exit code 2, so component was skipped. These were assertion failures, not a compile error. The fixer looked at the first 40 failures, and every one threw `ArgumentException: Unknown semantic matcher 'isuuid' ...`, with the name in lowercase. Most were in `SemanticMatcherTests` (e.g. `Apply_IsNullWithNullValue_Passes`, `Apply_IsEmailWithValidEmail_Passes`, `Apply_CustomMatcherName_DelegatesToCustomFunc`), plus one in `ResultMatcherTests` (`MatchResponseBody_NullExpectedWithSemanticMatcher_DoesNotThrow`).\n\n**Root cause:** In `src/ConfIT/Matching/SemanticMatcher.cs`, `ParseSpec` lowercased the matcher name (`ToLowerInvariant()`). But the `BuiltIns` dictionary uses camelCase keys like `isUuid` and `greaterThan`, and its lookups are case-sensitive. So no built-in matcher could ever be found, and neither could a custom matcher with uppercase letters in its name.\n\n**What was changed:** Only `ParseSpec` in `SemanticMatcher.cs`: the fixer removed the two `ToLowerInvariant()` calls (around lines 85 and 91), so names are looked up exactly as written. No tests were edited.\n\n**Notes:**\n- **Case-sensitivity:** Matcher names are now case-sensitive. If names were meant to work in any case (e.g. `isuuid`), the right fix is a case-insensitive comparer on `BuiltIns` and on the custom-matcher lookup. That change wasn't made.\n- **Not covered:** The checks that ran were build, unit and component. Integration tests and `make skills` aren't among them, so those weren't checked.\n\nNothing was committed.",
